WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.447
Week 31: Comparison, Register, and Idiomatic Force. make a nuanced comparison praise or criticize appropriately interpret idioms from context

2
00:00:10.447 --> 00:00:18.569
Cultural lens. Idioms are regional and register-bound; recognition matters more than indiscriminate production.

3
00:00:18.569 --> 00:00:30.641
Avoid the single-story trap. Treat country, region, age, relationship, and setting as variables. Describe the evidence you have without turning one example into a universal rule.

4
00:00:30.641 --> 00:00:42.499
Language variation. Recognize regional choices and ask what a form means locally. Use the neutral course model for production until the situation gives you a reason to adapt.

5
00:00:42.499 --> 00:00:51.498
Reflection. Compare this context with one you know. Name one similarity, one difference, and one question that would prevent an assumption.

6
00:00:51.498 --> 00:01:22.727
Model language. La segunda opción es mucho más viable. — The second option is much more viable. El informe menos claro fue el primero. — The least clear report was the first one. El proyecto va viento en popa. — The project is going very well. Ese dato hay que tomarlo con pinzas. — That figure must be treated with caution. Su análisis dio en el clavo; sin embargo, faltan pruebas. — The analysis was exactly right; however, evidence is still lacking.

7
00:01:22.727 --> 00:01:32.646
Check your readiness. Explain the core idea. Produce an original example. Complete the section assessment at 85% or higher.
